  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis depict mid/upper \nridging over the Desert Southwest with an upper trough over the \nGreat Lakes. At the surface, a stalled surface boundary spans across \nsouthern Nebraska into northwest Kansas. Temperatures throughout the \narea so far today have overachieved somewhat, with observed values \nin the 105-110 degree range as of early this afternoon. As parcels \nreach their convective temperatures later this afternoon and \nevening, isolated showers and storms are possible mainly along/north \nof I-70. This activity is not expected to be severe given modest \nflow aloft, though small hail and brief gusty winds may be possible \nwith the strongest storms. Furthermore, a few short-term models also \nhighlight the potential for a few elevated showers near the vicinity \nof the stalled surface boundary drifting from northwest Kansas \ninto our central counties heading into Monday morning. With \nconfidence being a bit low at this time owing to drier low level\nconditions, decided to introduce silent PoPs to account for \nthis potential.\n\nThe prevailing pattern will continue to promote hot temperatures \nthrough at least Wednesday. Layer thicknesses suggest areawide highs \nin the 100-105 degree range, so not currently expecting temperatures \nto be quite as hot as today. For Monday, fairly mixed-out dewpoints \ncould be just enough to keep heat indices below advisory criteria \nfor most locations. As such, conditions appear marginal enough to \nnot issue a heat advisory with this cycle, though this may change as \nadditional guidance becomes available. Similar conditions are \nforecast for Tuesday with highs ranging from 100 to 105 degrees. \nRegarding rain chances, little change in the positioning of the \naforementioned stationary front is anticipated as well, which may \nallow for continued isolated shower/storm chances mainly north of I-\n70 during the late afternoon and early evening hours Monday and \nTuesday.\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft is progged across the Plains beginning \nWednesday as a couple of waves arrive in the western CONUS. Embedded \nripples in flow tracking through the Rockies and into the Central \nPlains may allow for scattered showers and storms across central \nKansas Wednesday evening, though most guidance to this point places \nthe main activity further north into northern Kansas and southern \nNebraska. As such, another hot day with highs in the low to middle \n100s appears likely at this time.\n\nLooking toward the end of the week, there remains some question as \nto how far south additional rain chances make it. For Thursday and \npossibly even Friday, model trends to this point have shifted the \nlocation of the stationary boundary further north with each run, \nwhich decreases confidence in showers and storms providing much-\nneeded relief to the forecast area. This would support continued hot \ntemperatures especially across far southern Kansas heading into next \nweekend.",
